<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Guizot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1787">1787</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Guizot was a French historian and liberal politician who dominated the government of the July Monarchy in the 1840s, first as foreign minister and then as prime minister. As education minister his law of 1833 required every commune to keep a primary school. His refusal to widen the narrow electorate helped bring the revolution of February 1848 and the king's fall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-guizot.jpg" alt="François Guizot" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jehan Georges Vibert / After Paul Delaroche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guizot,_Fran%C3%A7ois_-_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>François Guizot was born on 4 October 1787 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> into a bourgeois Protestant family. His father was executed on the scaffold there on 8 April 1794, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>, when François was six. His mother took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, where he was educated, and in 1805 he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as a tutor. He began writing for the journal Publiciste and in 1812 married Pauline de Meulan, a fellow contributor. Under the Restoration he was one of the leaders of the Doctrinaires, a small party attached to the charter and the crown that favoured the juste milieu, a middle path between absolutism and popular government. In 1822 his lectures were banned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 he supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a>. In 1832 he became minister of public instruction and carried the law of 28 June 1833, which established primary education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He also revived the Académie des Sciences Morales et Politiques and founded the Société de l'histoire de France, which published medieval chronicles at the state's expense. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as ambassador in the spring of 1840. From 29 October 1840 he led the government for nearly eight years, as foreign minister and, after Marshal Soult retired, as prime minister from September 1847.</p><p>With Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> he built an understanding between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s ashes were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a>. Opponents called his foreign policy subservient to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he answered that they would never reach the height of his disdain. He was unpopular with the more advanced liberals for restricting the vote to propertied men. When he banned the reform banquets in January 1848, the opposition grew, and on 23 February 1848 the king told him he doubted he could keep him as prime minister. Guizot resigned and escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he arrived on 3 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He never returned to public life and spent most of each year at Val Richer in Normandy, supporting himself by writing. His works include histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> under Cromwell, the Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire de mon temps, and a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> told to his grandchildren. He was elected to the Académie Française in 1836. He died in 1874, after reciting verses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-corneille">Pierre Corneille</a> and texts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, according to report.</p><p>In the 1820s his Histoire générale de la civilisation en <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was admired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, Goethe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a>. His critics call him a conservative or reactionary for resisting universal suffrage, but he defended representative government against both absolutism and the excesses of democracy, and he called for the abolition of capital punishment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François Guizot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Government rested on a narrow electorate of wealthy men, and ministers such as <b>François Guizot</b> and Adolphe Thiers alternated in power.</blockquote><a href="/state/july-monarchy">July Monarchy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Abbey of Val-Richer, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.84,4.36&amp;year=1843" aria-label="Nîmes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of François Guizot"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 3823h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 3823h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.84,4.36&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
